course: biology
lesson: 263
title: The human body deeper: bones, muscles and skin
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Bones are not dead sticks like the skeleton in a classroom: they are alive, with blood vessels and cells that keep rebuilding them, which is why a broken arm in a cast slowly knits back together.
explain: A baby has about 300 bones, and many of them join together as it grows, so a grown-up ends up with 206.
explain: Muscles can only pull, never push, so they work in pairs: when you flex your arm, the biceps shortens and bulges to bend it, and the triceps on the back pulls to straighten it again.
explain: Your skin is your biggest organ, about 2 square metres on an adult, a living coat that keeps germs out, grows goosebumps in the cold and leaves fingerprints on the window.
explain: In cartoons like Tom and Jerry, Tom gets flattened like a pancake or shatters into pieces and pops back into shape a second later; real bones can break, and a broken bone needs a doctor, a cast and weeks of healing.
fact: An adult has 206 bones, but a baby has about 300, and many of them join together as the child grows.
fact: Bones are alive, with blood vessels and cells that rebuild them, so a broken bone can heal.
fact: The smallest bone, the stirrup in your ear, is smaller than a grain of rice.
fact: The funny bone is not a bone but a nerve near the elbow, close to the skin, and bumping it sends a zinging feeling down the arm; the name may be a joke on the arm bone called the humerus.
fact: You are about a centimetre taller in the morning, because the soft discs between your spine bones squash a little during the day and spring back at night; astronauts grow a few centimetres taller in space, where no gravity presses the discs down.
fact: Muscles can only pull, not push, so they work in pairs, like the biceps that bends the arm and the triceps that straightens it.
fact: There are over 600 muscles in the body, and the face alone has over 40, which make thousands of expressions.
fact: People often say smiling takes fewer muscles than frowning, but it depends on the smile, and there is no good evidence either way.
fact: The jaw muscle, the masseter, bites very hard for its size, the heart is the hardest-working muscle, and the biggest muscle is the gluteus maximus in your bottom.
fact: Skin is the biggest organ, about 2 square metres on an adult; sunburn damages skin cells, which the body then sheds, and sun cream and a hat protect skin from harmful ultraviolet rays.
fact: Goosebumps happen when tiny muscles pull hairs upright; in furry animals this fluffs up the fur to trap warm air or look bigger.
fact: Fingerprints are ridges that help us grip, every person's are different, even identical twins', and koalas have fingerprints very like ours.
fact: Fingers and toes wrinkle in the bath because nerves make them do it, perhaps for better grip in the wet, like tyre treads; a finger whose nerve is cut does not wrinkle.
fact: Bones need calcium, for example from milk, vitamin D from sunlight, and exercise such as running and jumping to stay strong; bones weaken when they carry no weight, so astronauts lose bone strength in space unless they exercise every day.
exercise: How many bones does an adult have? | 206 | 300 | 100 | 512
exercise: Why does a baby have more bones than a grown-up? | Many of its bones join together as it grows | Grown-ups lose bones when they fall | Babies have extra bones in their fingers for ever | Babies have two skeletons
exercise: Why can a broken bone heal? | Bones are alive, with cells that rebuild them | The plaster cast slowly grows new bone into the crack | Broken bones melt and set again | Muscles glue the pieces together
exercise: Where is the smallest bone in the body? | In the ear | In the little toe | In the nose | In the little finger
exercise: What is the «funny bone» really? | A nerve near the elbow | The smallest bone in the arm | A bone that makes you laugh | A muscle in the shoulder
exercise: When are you a little taller? | In the morning | In the evening | After lunch | After running
exercise: Why do astronauts grow a few centimetres in space? | Without gravity the discs in their spine are not squashed | Space food has extra calcium in it that makes bones grow longer | They stretch in the rocket | The cold air makes them longer
exercise: Why do muscles work in pairs? | Muscles can only pull, so another must pull the other way | One muscle always sleeps while the other one does all of the work | Two muscles are stronger than bones | Pairs of muscles share the same blood
exercise: What does the biceps do when you flex your arm? | It gets shorter and fatter as it pulls | It gets longer and thinner, like a stretched rubber band | It pushes the arm straight | It stays the same shape
exercise: About how many muscles are in the body? | Over 600 | About 60 | Exactly 206 | About 6,000
exercise: Does smiling really take fewer muscles than frowning? | It depends on the smile, and there is no good evidence either way | Yes, frowning takes exactly 10 more muscles, as scientists have counted | No, smiling takes 100 more | Yes, smiling takes no muscles at all
exercise: Which is the biggest muscle in the body? | The gluteus maximus in your bottom | The biceps in your arm | The heart | The tongue
exercise: Which muscle works hardest, never resting? | The heart | The masseter | The biceps | The calf
exercise: About how big is an adult's skin? | About 2 square metres | About the size of a stamp | About 20 square metres | About the size of a football pitch
exercise: What causes goosebumps? | Tiny muscles pulling hairs upright | Cold air freezing the top of the skin into lumps | Bubbles of air under the skin | Little bumps of fat moving up
exercise: Which animal has fingerprints very like ours? | The koala | The cat | The parrot | The frog
exercise: Why may fingers wrinkle in the bath? | Nerves make them wrinkle, perhaps for better grip in the wet | The skin soaks up so much water that it bursts open and folds | The bath water is too hot | The fingers are getting old
exercise: What do bones need to stay strong? | Calcium, vitamin D and exercise | Only sugar and rest | Lots of salt and sleep | Nothing, bones never change
test: In a cartoon, Tom is flattened like a pancake by a steamroller and pops back into shape a second later. What is true for a real body? | Bones can break, and healing takes a doctor, a cast and weeks | Bodies spring back into shape like rubber, after any squash at all | Only the skin gets flat, the bones bend back | Bones can never break, only bend
test: «whacked my funny bone, why's my whole arm buzzing if it's just a bone» What is right? | It is a nerve, not a bone, and squeezing it sends a zing down the arm | The bone is hollow inside and rings like a bell when you hit it hard | The arm bone cracked a little | The blood stopped for a second
test: Count them: Mia's cat has 4 legs and Mia has 2 arms and 2 legs. In the morning Mia measures herself. How many legs are in the room? | 6 | 4 | 8 | 2
test: «my mate reckons twins have the same fingerprints so police can't tell 'em apart» What is right? | No, even identical twins have different fingerprints | Yes, identical twins share fingerprints | Yes, but only on their thumbs | No, twins have no fingerprints at all
test: What would happen if the biceps had no partner muscle? | You could bend your arm but not straighten it again by muscle | You could not bend your arm at all, not even a tiny bit, ever | The arm would bend both ways | The arm would grow much stronger
test: A frightened cat puffs up its fur. Which part of you does the same job? | The goosebumps on your arms | The fingerprints on your hands | The wrinkles on your fingers | The nails on your toes
test: «got burnt at the beach, now skin's peeling off, gross» What is happening? | The body is shedding skin cells the sun damaged | The skin is growing a new colour underneath the old one | Sand is coming out of the skin | The skin is turning into a tan
test: «strongest muscle's the tongue, everybody knows that» What is a better answer? | It depends what «strongest» means: the jaw bites hardest, the heart works hardest, the bottom is biggest | Yes, the tongue lifts the most weight | No, the strongest muscle is the eyelid | Yes, the tongue never gets tired
test: Count them: in the hospital 3 children each have an X-ray showing 2 broken bones. How many broken bones are there in all? | 6 | 5 | 9 | 3
test: Why would living with no gravity weaken an astronaut's bones? | Bones get weaker when they do not carry weight, unless astronauts exercise | Space air dissolves bones | Astronauts drink no milk at all, so their bones go soft and bendy in a few days | Bones float away from the muscles
test: Why might wrinkly wet fingers have helped our ancestors? | They may grip better on wet rocks and in streams | They keep the fingers warmer | They stop water getting in | They make hands look older and wiser
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the muscles of the arm are arranged in opposing pairs. | Muscles are capable only of pulling, and not of pushing | One muscle of each pair is invariably at rest while the other labours | A single muscle is insufficient to support a bone | The pairs are required to share a single blood supply
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number of bones in the human body. | An infant possesses approximately three hundred bones, many of which fuse, so that an adult possesses two hundred and six | An infant and an adult possess an identical number of bones, the bones merely increasing in size as the body grows | An adult possesses approximately three hundred bones | An infant possesses no bones until the age of one year
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the so-called «funny bone»? | It is a nerve situated close to the skin near the elbow | It is the smallest bone within the ear | It is a muscle of the upper arm | It is the bone of the upper arm termed the humerus
exam: Ascertain the cause of the slight difference in a person's height between morning and evening. | The compression during the day of the discs between the bones of the spine | The growth of the legs during sleep, which is then reversed during the hours of the day | The shrinking of the skull in the evening | The drying of the skin over the course of the day
